# A place centred at an affine point is the point's place

Theorem statement:
`AlgebraicCurve.Place.eq_ofHeightOneSpectrum_of_XClass_mem_nonunits_of_YClass_mem_nonunits`
(`719fe46e-a7ed-5a9e-b7d7-c19777574935`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `9d89dec3-40df-5919-86a2-b424db66fe70`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $F$ be a field and let $W$ be an affine Weierstrass curve over $F$ whose coordinate ring $F[W]
=$ `W.CoordinateRing` is a Dedekind domain, and let $x, y \in F$. Let $v$ be a place of the function
field $W.FunctionField$ over $F$, that is: a valuation subring $\mathcal{O}_v$ of the function field
which contains the image of $F$ under the structure map, is not the whole function field, and is a
principal ideal ring. Assume that the images in the function field of the two classes
$\mathrm{XClass}\,W\,x$ (the class of $X - x$) and $\mathrm{YClass}\,W\,(C\,y)$ (the class of $Y -
y$) in $F[W]$ both lie in the set of non-units of $\mathcal{O}_v$, i.e. in its maximal ideal. Let
$w$ be a height-one prime of $F[W]$ whose underlying ideal is $\mathrm{XYIdeal}\,W\,x\,(C\,y)$, the
ideal generated by those two classes. Then $v$ equals the place attached to $w$, whose valuation
subring is the valuation subring of the $w$-adic valuation on the function field.

This is the statement that a place of the function field whose valuation ring has $X - x$ and $Y -
y$ in its maximal ideal — a place centred at the affine point $(x,y)$ — is the place given by the
order of vanishing at the corresponding height-one prime of the coordinate ring. It is used to
identify the place attached to an affine point of a Weierstrass curve with an adic place, in
[`WeierstrassCurve.Affine.eq_placeOfPoint_some_of_XClass_mem_nonunits_of_YClass_mem_nonunits`]
(thm.html#WeierstrassCurve.Affine.
eq_placeOfPoint_some_of_XClass_mem_nonunits_of_YClass_mem_nonunits),
[`WeierstrassCurve.Affine.placeOfPoint_some_eq_ofHeightOneSpectrum`]
(thm.html#WeierstrassCurve.Affine.placeOfPoint_some_eq_ofHeightOneSpectrum)
and in the comparison of places under a variable change.

